+| `persist.gammaos.shader.4x3.window.<package>` | bool | The emulator draws its game into its window, not a SurfaceView (default on for RetroArch) |
+
+## 4:3 Cropped (`4x3-cropped`)
+
+Unlike the other types, this is not a post-process. SurfaceFlinger picks the game layer of the
+emulator Nano launched (`sys.gammaos.nano.launch_app`) on each display, whatever it shows; the
+shader is turned off for a game it doesn't suit. The game area (`gammaCropProjection`) is the
+picture (the layer's buffer, in its own pixels) when that is within 5% of 4:3, or else its largest
+centred 4:3 rectangle, where an emulator such as Dolphin letterboxes its game. A marked layer is
+client composited. RetroArch draws its game into its window, so it is filtered as if
+`persist.gammaos.shader.4x3.window.<package>` were set (the property can turn that off); its
+own menu, drawn in the same window, is projected with the game.
+
+RenderEngine draws a marked layer through `GammaSurfaceScaler`: the game area fills an 800 × 600
+projection on the panel (1600 × 1200 on a 1080p display), one display pixel per projected pixel,
+centred and cropped to the display, with black around it. On the panel that cuts 40 projected
+pixels from each side and leaves 60 black rows above and below; at 1080p it cuts 60 rows from the
+top and bottom and leaves 160 black columns each side. Sampling is sharp bilinear: each texel is
+held flat and blended with its neighbour only across the display pixel on their shared edge. When
+the picture is shrunk the kernel is plain bilinear.
+
+The game layer is the launched package's SurfaceView, then its opaque buffer layer, then the one
+with the largest area, so a SurfaceView wins over the translucent window above it. A window that is
+opaque outside its SurfaceView still covers the extended picture there.
+
+## 8:7 Cropped (`8x7-cropped`)
+
+4:3 Cropped stretched vertically (`GammaCrop::k8x7`): the same 4:3 game area, or a picture within
+5% of 8:7 such as a core's raw 256 × 224 frame, fills an 800 × 720 projection on the panel (40 projected pixels cut from
+each side, as 4:3 Cropped) or 1200 × 1080 on a 1080p display. The game layer
+choice and the exclusions are the same; RetroArch's copy is `rg_cube_8_7_cropped`.
 
